title: Wires inside: signals
check: behaves
---- intro
In a drawing, a wire between two gates has no name. In VHDL it needs one: a **signal**. You declare it between `architecture` and `begin`:

```
signal either : std_logic;
```

and then use it like any input or output: give it a value with `<=`, and read it in other lines.

The starter declares two signals for the XOR you built from AND, OR and NOT: `either` (a OR b) and `both` (a AND b).
---- task
Build XOR from AND, OR and NOT using the two signals: set `either` and `both`, then make `y` "either, and not both".
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity my_xor is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end my_xor;

architecture rtl of my_xor is
    signal either, both : std_logic;
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity my_xor is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end my_xor;

architecture rtl of my_xor is
    signal either, both : std_logic;
begin
    either <= a or b;
    both   <= a and b;
    y      <= either and not both;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Three lines: one gives `either` its value, one gives `both` its value, and one makes `y` out of the two.
---- hint2
`either <= a or b;`, `both <= a and b;` and `y <= either and not both;`
---- solution
```
either <= a or b;
both   <= a and b;
y      <= either and not both;
```
Each signal is a wire with a name; the drawing shows the four gates you built by hand in the Circuits lessons.
---- done
Signals let you build big circuits out of named pieces, the same way chips did in the drawing. Real designs have hundreds of them.
